<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Latest news from the Middle East, daily international news reports - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage and analyses of political and social issues across the Middle East, including Egypt, the Gulf states, Iraq, Israel, Jordan, Lebanon, Saudi Arabia, Syria, Yemen and beyond.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>West Bank: settlers using drones to monitor Palestinians</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dbf53ac906655f8dadbdb095503ca15a2850a0474af875c46725b137da2818f8</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T13:39:26Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20261028-west-bank-settlers-using-drones-to-monitor-palestinians" /><summary type="html">"We feel like we’re being watched all the time." In the occupied West Bank, Palestinian farmers are speaking out against the extensive use of drones by Israeli settlers and the Israeli military. According to activists and researchers, this practice is aimed, in part, at monitoring new Palestinian construction. In 2026, the Israeli government allocated a budget of 50 million shekels (over 14 million euros) to outposts and illegal settlements in the West Bank to fund so-called "security" equipment—which includes these drones.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>'No clear winner' from Israeli election, expert says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0f10ddcabb672ba96c203015cfc8f53759f48d9cd71f1dd2b31f05b30f762786</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T12:49:36Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261028-no-clear-winner-from-israeli-election-expert-says" /><summary type="html">In this edition of Perspective, we focus on the results of the election in Israel. For analysis, we speak to Yossi Mekelberg, senior consulting fellow at Chatham House's Middle East and North Africa Programme. He tells us there is "no clear winner" from the country's pivotal vote.</summary><category term="Perspective" /></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: PM loses a significant number of seats since 2022 election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:095186e434f70c5267f5d11371959a50135d48d6d5ea1afd90bdcadb5c3d16e6</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T11:56:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-pm-loses-a-significant-number-of-seats-since-2022-election" /><summary type="html">Israel was counting votes on Wednesday after pivotal elections in which exit polls showed no clear winner. Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s bloc appears to have lost a significant number of seats compared with the previous election in 2022. France 24’s International Affairs Editor Angela Diffley shares further insights.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: As votes are counted, no clear winner emerges</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6bbde6305333f1e2896e04fd322fb1ca64524a1678cfff2b6891f518a4222955</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T11:32:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israeli-election-as-votes-are-counted-no-clear-winner-emerges" /><summary type="html">Anxious Israelis watched election results take shape Wednesday after the highest turnout in over a quarter-century, as no clear winner emerged and weeks of negotiating could lie ahead for Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and an array of opposition parties trying to unseat him. France 24 correspondent in Jerusalem Noga Tarnopolsky reports the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: Netanyahu edges closer to a majority</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1715f3cd3dd7d2ecf86b9f46e1bcc1597e21ccc28441c030121ad3827cde89a0</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T10:20:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-netanyahu-edges-closer-to-a-majority" /><summary type="html">The final results are expected tonight or tomorrow morning. For now, the picture is looking increasingly favourable for Benjamin Netanyahu and his bloc, but the Israeli prime minister is still short of a clear victory, as he needs 61 seats to secure a parliamentary majority. He addressed his supporters at the National Congress Centre at around 3 a.m. local time. France 24’s Clovis Casali joins us live from Jerusalem with the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: 'Wishful thinking' for pro- and anti-Netanyahu parties?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:617f22099178167e3f808228240f937e3c7f3a3e77acfeec24d8ff9fa33d007b</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T10:15:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261028-israeli-election-wishful-thinking-for-pro-and-anti-netanyahu-parties" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Wednesday, October 28: Papers react to the ongoing election results in Israel. Next: Macklemore's Free Palestine tour is coming to Paris. Also: antidepressants don't work the way we thought. Finally: a sneak peek into boomer divorcées' dating lives.</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>Israelis await final election results as Netanyahu remains far from defeated</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fb43ace74e03d4d41ec4676681649de3f0e7e0c4cd7d661a0b2d3d5dd7a7c0a5</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T10:12:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israelis-await-final-election-results-as-netanyahu-remains-far-from-defeated" /><summary type="html">Arab-led parties appear to have made a historic showing in the Israeli election, but it’s not clear if other opponents of Netanyahu's government would form a coalition with them for a majority in the next parliament. Issues around the Palestinians and their descendants remain incredibly sensitive in Israel.
 
France 24 24's International Affairs Commentator Douglas Herbert shares further insights.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: The economic challenges ahead</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:330385126165203d91e7f0074154c151e62cd8b456a4846db60c119ccd7abc6d</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:43:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-the-economic-challenges-ahead" /><summary type="html">Questions over the future of Israel’s economy are looming in the wake of the elections. With one of the highest costs of living in the region, and defence spending continuing to rise, households are facing growing financial pressure. Caitlin Brown reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel election: There's no clear winner</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:009a7a4efeaeb422a889189d10088a8393ecc2df1834858f3f0aeb38ac80d5c8</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:40:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-election-there-s-no-clear-winner" /><summary type="html">The path ahead remains uncertain in the Knesset in Jerusalem, as the Central Elections Committee continues to count the votes. With more than 60% of ballots tallied, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s camp is narrowly ahead of the opposition, but still short of a clear majority. 

These are still partial results and could change, meaning no clear winner has emerged so far. Both leading candidates for the prime minister’s office traded barbs in their respective speeches after polls closed. Antonia Kerrigan reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: Netanyahu and Eisenkot both pledge to form government</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2158698a168dfa156147c54409e7360b14ad985c5451d315d4df3ce20cd4e939</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:06:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-israel-netanyahu-and-eisenkot-both-pledge-to-form-government" /><summary type="html">🗳️ Both Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin #Netanyahu and rival Gadi #Eisenkot pledged to form a government as the two are projected to finish neck and neck in #Israel's critical election Tuesday. 
 
Exit polls show both blocs falling short of a parliamentary majority.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel election: Netanyahu and Eisenkot battle to form government after deadlock</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ac54b78f78b13e29c48b9dd49f5ca1aa8b580bed2fbf983c70f408cfb6c44acd</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T22:58:53Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261027-israel-election-record-turnout-netanyahu-eisenkot-deadlock" /><summary type="html">Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and rival Gadi Eisenkot on Wednesday both pledged to form a government after an election left neither bloc with a parliamentary majority. Record voter turnout followed a divisive campaign dominated by the October 7, 2023 attack, while Arab Israeli parties made significant gains.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Israel: First exit polls show no clear winner, Arab parties make gains</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d94e27953da4992d3235d66854d938a45839d447a125250fc581b70198af2301</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T21:41:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-israel-first-exit-polls-show-no-clear-winner-arab-parties-make-gains" /><summary type="html">Exit polls on Tuesday showed that the opposition bloc led by Israeli opposition candidate Gadi Eisenkot gained a narrow edge over Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's Likud bloc in the Knesset. 
 
Likud, however, was projected to be the largest single party in the Israeli parliament.</summary></entry><entry><title>Bibi's moment of truth: Israel votes in first general election since October 7 attacks</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5ab7a4db92665974a10d24f4a06d7f74c62a00167995da2d3d9783180ce5ca8e</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T21:26:10Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261027-bibi-s-moment-of-truth-israel-votes-in-first-general-election-since-october-7" /><summary type="html">The turnout tells the tale. Israelis are casting ballots in their largest numbers in decades in the first general election since the October 7 attacks of 2023. The vote is seen as a referendum on the bloodiest day in the nation's history. But more broadly, what kind of Israel do citizens want?</summary><category term="The Debate" /></entry><entry><title>Netanyahu govt has 'attacked' Israeli judicial system, Supreme Court and democratic institutions</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:be6d8ebdde72ed04941927494eccfea02c81e7adb41a415a3511ede8676716da</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T19:44:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-govt-has-attacked-israeli-judicial-system-supreme-court-and-democratic-institutions" /><summary type="html">Annette Young is pleased to welcome Alain Greilsammer, Professor of Political Science at Bar-Ilan University in Tel Aviv. In the wake of the October 7 attacks, regional tensions with Iran, and deepening domestic divisions, Greilsammer argues that the election will be fundamentally shaped by concerns over democratic accountability, judicial independence and the unequal distribution of civic obligations. He warns Netanyahu’s policies risk transforming Israel into “something like an authoritarian or non-democratic state.” He suggests that Israel’s Arab political parties could play a decisive role in determining the election’s outcome.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ibrahim Abu Ahmad on Israeli election: 'Crucial for Arab citizens to have ally in Jewish community'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4efd09c696d93ffed15990b1d0a13b55ade0f527c8d1802d4e8c299659b2805d</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T18:59:43Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-ibrahim-abu-ahmad-on-israeli-election-crucial-for-arab-citizens-to-have-ally-in-jewish-community" /><summary type="html">Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Ibrahim Abu Ahmad, Author, Palestinian Israeli political and peace activist and Head of Social Media for Israel's left-wing Democrats. “Currently, the sentiment in Israel is built around fear and trauma,” explains Ahmad. His most striking observation concerns the apparent contradiction between political leadership and public sentiment: “Many want to see an Arab-Jewish coalition. It's the leadership that is afraid to say it.” Ultimately, he moves beyond conventional electoral calculations to frame Arab-Jewish partnership as a question of democratic survival.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli elections a 'referendum' on Netanyahu, security and whether Israel will 'remain a democracy'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1ea45c9ca1ca6d3811d64f50c4e046795f0e0e6d89e75d4e57c117ac530de881</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T18:34:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israeli-elections-a-referendum-on-netanyahu-security-and-whether-israel-will-remain-a-democracy" /><summary type="html">Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Ron Gerlitz, CEO of aChord Center at Hebrew University, an Israeli organization specialising in the social psychology of intergroup relations. “The main issue in this election is the fight for Israeli democracy,” explains Gerlitz. And beneath the struggle over democratic institutions lies the erosion of Benjamin Netanyahu's longstanding association with national security. Gerlitz also examines the psychological foundations of Netanyahu's enduring support, the influence of conspiracy narratives, the electoral significance of Arab parties and the uncertain future of the two-state solution.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israelis vote in a tense election: What's at stake?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5a7da960dfae3dbcd0261edab7095c9099c2e3c5ef9a00044f13fa5fbbd37da7</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T16:59:06Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israelis-vote-in-a-tense-election-what-s-at-stake" /><summary type="html">Israelis were voting Tuesday (October 27) in a tense national election widely seen as a referendum on Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's leadership since the Hamas-led Oct. 7, 2023, attack on Israel and the wars that have since shaken the Middle East.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel elections: voter turnout reaches 55% by 4pm local time</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cc2a6259e774975586b0520f9604093da96a3d1fe633e2547aabda34f9f31e34</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T16:52:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-elections-voter-turnout-reaches-55-by-4pm-local-time" /><summary type="html">Israelis flocked to the polls Tuesday (October 27) in turnout unseen in decades, with a deeply divided electorate delivering a verdict on veteran right-wing Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu in the first election since the October 7, 2023 attack. FRANCE24's Kethevane Gorjestani tells us more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel votes in decisive election: What happens after polls close?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:63f3865b7f6e1d933b3fef42684839506ab76bf921b9c23fa5d6b83e5ef88a58</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T14:55:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-israel-s-unique-political-system-what-happens-after-polls-close" /><summary type="html">Israelis are voting in a pivotal election Tuesday (October 27) as their country wrestles with unresolved wars, continues to reel from Hamas' 2023 attack and is deeply divided over the character of the state and the right person to lead it. Details by FRANCE 24's International Affairs Editor, Angela Diffley.</summary></entry><entry><title>Turnout high as Israeli voters deliver verdict on Netanyahu</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a1b4d0911609b434dade3f6bb626715dbcd00b96c0e95b5a6b98545a723d10f2</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T14:45:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/turnout-high-as-israeli-voters-deliver-verdict-on-netanyahu" /><summary type="html">Israelis flocked to the polls Tuesday (October 27) in turnout unseen in decades, with a deeply divided electorate delivering a verdict on Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu in the first election since the October 7, 2023 attack. Former FRANCE 24 correspondent, Irris Makler, tells us more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: Netanyahu supporters gather as PM votes</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:54a985ea65dc771bef044288f801eedd660ada563e5f61dc6ca8dceded1c75f3</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T14:37:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israel-netanyahu-supporters-gather-as-pm-votes--3f57cd1da13b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-netanyahu-supporters-gather-as-pm-votes" /><summary type="html">Supporters of Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu gathered outside a polling station where the leader was voting on Tuesday. This is Israel's first election since the October7, 2023 Hamas attack and is seen as a referendum on Netanyahu. FRANCE 24's Clovis Casali reports from Jerusalem.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: A referendum on 'Bibi' Netanyahu?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fb8cc31eb61c6f0d8830bffd0468b611cffd1dd3eb7f0d05727b001792cd0f6e</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T12:52:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261027-israeli-election-a-referendum-on-bibi-netanyahu--42870793125e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261027-israeli-election-a-referendum-on-bibi-netanyahu" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Tuesday, October 27: The Israeli, Palestinian and Lebanese papers break down what's at stake on election day for Israel and its neighbours. Also, the French government announces its first major concessions to protesting high school students, hoping to calm the movement. Finally, Harry Kane's Ballon d'Or means Lamine Yamal and Kylian Mbappé will have to wait their turn.</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>Israeli PM casts his ballot as voters voice opposition to Netanyahu outside polling stations</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1329bb8d1c9f3dd9f88077cb3441aa01da9a7629180220a0d58ea51240ec6d50</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T12:42:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israeli-pm-casts-his-ballot-as-voters-voice-opposition-to-netanyahu-outside-polling-stations--664441f7283c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israeli-pm-casts-his-ballot-as-voters-voice-opposition-to-netanyahu-outside-polling-stations" /><summary type="html">Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu cast his vote on Tuesday (October 27) in Israel’s general election. FRANCE 24's Clovis Casali tells us more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Iraqi court sentences six French nationals to life for IS group membership</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b53011054b461cd82064092117ded062bfcd4d03d9db9347bfcaa4f4b7c8d416</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T11:16:38Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261027-iraqi-court-sentences-six-french-nationals-to-life-for-is-group-membership--3e5d4146faaa.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261027-iraqi-court-sentences-six-french-nationals-to-life-for-is-group-membership" /><summary type="html">Six French men were sentenced to life in prison by an Iraqi court on Tuesday for membership of the Islamic State (IS) group, according to a source. They are ​among ‌47 French IS group suspects transferred from ⁠detention facilities in Syria to Iraq last year under an arrangement coordinated by US forces and Iraqi authorities.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Israeli election: Fragile co-existence between residents in Jewish-Arab town of Lod</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3353aa258d49757ec7259437c64df7a1d4602d52016a39c3f7b7ff0dac074e70</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T11:14:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261027-israeli-election-fragile-co-existence-between-residents-in-jewish-arab-town-of-lod--de028a4ce766.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261027-israeli-election-fragile-co-existence-between-residents-in-jewish-arab-town-of-lod" /><summary type="html">As Israelis head to the polls this Tuesday, among those voting are 2 million Palestinian citizens of Israel, some of whom live in mixed Jewish-Arab towns. One of them, Lod, has long been seen as symbolic of this coexistence. However in 2021 the town was the scene of violent clashes, whose consequences are still being felt. FRANCE 24's Stéphane Amar, Sandra Lederer and Josh Vardey report.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>Syria slams deadly Jordan strikes as ‘unacceptable’ breach of airspace</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0b2e3636e9d14ea665eeaebd55ce8053f830071a2fdb60829772ad688951b29d</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T11:02:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261027-syria-slams-deadly-jordan-strikes-as-unacceptable-breach-of-airspace--2b9a8e2f5e25.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261027-syria-slams-deadly-jordan-strikes-as-unacceptable-breach-of-airspace" /><summary type="html">Syria's foreign ministry on Tuesday condemned Jordanian strikes in southern Syria in retaliation for a deadly church attack in the kingdom last week that Amman linked to militants operating across the border. Syria said the strikes killed seven ⁠people, including three children and a woman. Jordan said it regretted the civilian casualties.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Jordan launches airstrikes in Syria after church attack</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:110e18a89835e42b85bdff4ab07ad727ad2ebf1468d4e817a76cc7f82deb588f</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T10:59:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/jordan-launches-airstrikes-in-syria-after-church-attack--27982e6f3f71.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/jordan-launches-airstrikes-in-syria-after-church-attack" /><summary type="html">Jordan carried out airstrikes Monday (October 27) evening against armed groups in Syria in retaliation for Friday’s attack on a Greek Orthodox Church in the southern Jordanian city of Karak that killed nine people, the Jordanian air force said in a statement. Syrian authorities said at least six people were killed.</summary></entry><entry><title>Palestinians in West Bank fear further escalation of violence if Netanyahu remains PM</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c93b210df9b52e587fac2602c0d2d2f4c19d1723d864843e234e7112515bd6ef</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T10:50:46Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/palestinians-in-west-bank-fear-further-escalation-of-violence-if-netanyahu-remains-pm--1f5e2d22638e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/palestinians-in-west-bank-fear-further-escalation-of-violence-if-netanyahu-remains-pm" /><summary type="html">Ramallah residents say they hope to see Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu lose power in elections. "Netanyahu remaining in power means that there will be a clash, a build-up of tension and an explosion in the region", says one resident. Israel has committed the crime against humanity of "extermination" in the West Bank, while continuing to commit "genocide" against Palestinians in the Gaza Strip, United Nations-mandated investigators said Monday (October 27).</summary></entry><entry><title>Israelis vote in first national election since October 7 attack</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4972b6d921db56ebc47417fa1a39982ed5937f97bd414330fb73b197b42cf52d</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T10:41:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israelis-vote-in-first-national-election-since-october-7-attack--c484a521ac8a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israelis-vote-in-first-national-election-since-october-7-attack" /><summary type="html">Israelis are voting in a pivotal election Tuesday (October 27) as their country wrestles with unresolved wars, continues to reel from Hamas' 2023 attack and is deeply divided over the character of the state and the right person to lead it. FRANCE 24's Clovis Casali tells us more from the ground. He spoke with voters.</summary></entry><entry><title>Netanyahu's political survival on the line as Israel heads to the polls</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:af57efa9af97e68f0b1369269635b5a5fd611c43cf7d9738b8aa793f68369467</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:13:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T10:14:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-s-political-survival-on-the-line-as-israel-heads-to-the-polls--b5fbf6bedec9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/netanyahu-s-political-survival-on-the-line-as-israel-heads-to-the-polls" /><summary type="html">Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu faces a fight for political survival in Israel's election on Tuesday (October 27), with voters weighing his leadership after the 2023 Hamas-led attacks, wars in Gaza and with Iran, and Israel's deepening international isolation. FRANCE24's Douglas Herbert gives us his analysis.</summary></entry></feed>
